watchdog: sunxi_wdt: stop for system sleep, feed while userspace is frozen

With the right register layout the watchdog finally acts, and a standby
then meets it twice (crust-20261007 review, TFA-6, INT-06):

- it keeps counting through the sleep (HY310, run A4: reset 16.4 s after
  arming, across a 5 s light standby), and 16 s is its maximum;
- the one who feeds it, systemd's RuntimeWatchdogSec, is frozen from
  before the device suspend until after the device resume, and freezing
  alone may take 20 s (kernel/power/process.c).

The core does not help: its PM notifier is registered only with
WDOG_NO_PING_ON_SUSPEND and would only ping once.

So the driver takes the watchdog over for exactly that stretch:

- PM_SUSPEND_PREPARE: if it runs, feed it now and then every 4 s (at
  most half the timeout), for at most standby_keepalive_s (30 s, held to
  1..120 s, the cap crust puts on its own budgets);
- suspend_noirq: if it runs, stop the feeding, feed once, stop it and say
  "stopped for standby";
- resume_noirq: if it was stopped there, program it afresh (timeout,
  CFG, MODE - after a deep standby the block may have lost all three),
  feed it, feed again for at most standby_keepalive_s, "re-armed";
- PM_POST_SUSPEND (userspace is thawed by then): stop the feeding.

A kernel that hangs in device suspend or resume is therefore reset
standby_keepalive_s plus the timeout later; the noirq lines carry the
timestamps that show where the stop and the re-arm fell. This also covers
s2idle, which never reaches TF-A. noirq is the place, not
PM_SUSPEND_PREPARE: stopping there would drop the net over the whole
device suspend and resume, where the hangs of runs A1-A4 were. An arisc
driver that hands the sleep to crust has to order its own noirq step
against this one with a device link to the node named in
allwinner,watchdog; the arisc driver in the tree today does not.

Whether re-arming after a long sleep is safe rests on the reload in
sunxi_wdt_start and the feed right after it; whether a stopped block's
count keeps running, and whether the block fires at once when it is
enabled past its interval, is not measured.

W2 of the crust release design. Compiled out of tree with W=1; not
measured on the device.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
